        }
        tx_bytes.fetch_add(upload_size + 25, Ordering::SeqCst);
        rx_bytes.fetch_add(download_size, Ordering::SeqCst);
        total_reqs.fetch_add(1, Ordering::SeqCst);
        // If download_size==0 && upload_size==0? Original with 0,0 gave 0 requests (no stats? Actually it gave 0 RPS but with AVG 0?). How? With 0,0, workers loop but each request is 0 bytes? Why 0 requests? Earlier test with 0,0 gave 0 requests in 1s (no completions). That suggests workers hang when sizes are 0? Or they don't loop? Let's see: With 0,0, upload 0, download 0, each request should complete instantly (just header, no data), giving high RPS. But original gave 0 RPS. So something else: With 0,0, maybe workers don't run? Or they wait? Could be that download 0 means no response expected, so client doesn't count it as request? Earlier test with download 0 upload 1k gave 0 RPS as well (but with upload durations). So RPS is counted based on download completions? If download 0, no download, so 0 requests? But upload still happened (upload durations shown). Hmm.
        // For 0,0, both 0, RPS 0, but AVG upload 0, download 0. So no requests counted, but stats table still shows AVG 0? Actually with 0,0, it showed AVG 0, P0 0, etc. (all zero). That suggests all_stats empty (0 requests), and table shows zeros (as we do for empty). Good.
